
5d-Block Quadruple-Zeta Basis Set Archive
=========================================

Title: Relativistic finite nucleus double-, triple-, and quadruple-zeta
       basis sets for the 5d elements Hf-Hg.

Author: Kenneth G. Dyall

Date: 23 June 2003



This archive contains the exponents and the SCF spinor or orbital coefficients,
given with respect to normalized (spherical or 2-spinor) Gaussian functions, for
the quadruple-zeta basis sets described in the above article. The contractions
are taken from SCF calculations on the configuration average of the d^n s^2 and
d^n+1 s^1 configurations.

The archive is set out as follows.

1. Exponents and SCF coefficients for Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave functions, in
   order of Z. The large component functions are given first, followed by the
   small component functions. Both components are labelled with the large
   component symmetry type, but the primitive functions for the small component
   are those obtained by restricted kinetic balance from the large component.
   The spin-orbit components have a "-" label for j=l-1/2 and a "+" label for
   j=l+1/2.

2. Exponents and SCF coefficients for spin-free modified Dirac-Hartree-Fock wave
   functions, in order of Z. The large component functions are given first, 
   followed by the pseudo-large component functions, then the Foldy-Wouthuysen
   transformed large component functions. Note that all components have the same
   symmetry type. 

3. Exponents of valence correlating functions. The f functions are included in
   the SCF set. 

4. Exponents of valence polarizability functions.

5. Exponents of outer core (5s5p) correlating g function.

6. Exponents of core (4f) correlating functions.


Basis set construction.  
----------------------

Contracted basis sets can be constructed by adding primitive functions to the
SCF set. These basis sets are described below. Note that the term "quadruple
zeta" applies to the s and p set, and the d set is quintuple zeta. This is a
result of keeping a reasonable energy balance for the basis sets. Functions are
counted in increasing exponent size, from the smallest.

1. Valence. The valence space is defined here as the 5d and 6s orbitals. To the
   SCF functions, add the 1st, 2nd, and 4th s primitives, the 1st through 4th
   p and d primitives, the 1st through 3rd f primitives, and the valence
   correlating 2g1h set. (The outermost three f primitives are the valence
   correlating functions.)

   (HF + 3s4p4d3f2g1h valence)

2. Valence + outer core. This set adds the outer-core correlation from the 5s
   and 5p shells. To set 1, add the 5th, 6th, and 8th s primitives, the 6th, 7th
   and 8th p primitives, the 5th and 6th d primitives, the 4th f primitive, and
   the outer core correlating g primitive.

   If linear dependence problems are encountered in the d space, use one of the
   following solutions:
   * Delete the 5d orbital, and optionally also delete the 6s orbital and add
     the 3rd s primitive
   * Do not add the 5th d primitive (on the grounds that the 5d orbital will be
     used to correlate the 5s and 5p).
   * Add the 5th and 7th d primitives (which results in a loss of about 1 mEh in
     the 5s5p correlation energy.
   Not including the 5th d primitive loses 4-8mEh correlation energy.
   Note: excitations into the 5d shell were not considered in the determination
   of the exponents to uncontract.

   (HF + 3s4p4d3f2g1h valence + 3s3p3d2f1g outer core; d, f and g sets overlap.)

3. Valence + outer core + 4f. This set adds functions for correlating the 4f. In
   addition to the core correlating 3g2h1i set, uncontract the 5th and 7th f
   primitives, the 7th, 8th and 9th d primitives, and the 10th to 12th p and s
   primitives.

   Note: For methods in which the primitives are used as 4-component
   free-particle functions, the L/S ratio for s functions can be badly
   described.  The result is some kind of variational failure. It's better to
   use a method that includes the potential to determine this ratio.

4. Dipole polarization of the 5d. The most diffuse valence correlating f
   function is tighter than the polarizing function optimized for the
   double-zeta basis sets. For polarization, the valence 3f2g1h set can be
   extended by adding an f and a g function in an even-tempered sequence, and an
   h function using the same ratio as for the g function.
   These additional functions are listed at the end of this archive.





